+namespace base::xrio {
+
+// The identity this build claims. Values come from personas/ and from the knob
+// registry in xrio_knobs.h -- real captures from our own hardware and the
+// literals they used to be compiled as, never invented constants.
+//
+// EVERY PREDICATE HERE IS A KNOB NOW. It used to be that five of them were
+// `return true` with no way to reach them, which meant a five-hour rebuild to
+// answer "what breaks if we turn this off". The defaults reproduce that
+// behaviour exactly, so nothing changes for a launch that passes no flags.
+//
+// TWO ACTIVATION POLICIES, and the split is deliberate.
+//
+// GATED, defaulting to OFF: the GL table, the renderer and vendor strings, the
+// extension filter, readback perturbation, the machine profile. For these a
+// wrong value is worse than no value -- claiming to be a RADV RENOIR while the
+// capability table underneath says SwiftShader is a contradiction no real driver
+// produces. Unconfigured means untouched.
+//
+// UNCONDITIONAL, defaulting to ON: navigator.webdriver, the Headless UA token,
+// the UA-CH brand. These invert the risk. Their default state is the tell:
+// shipping `webdriver === true`, a `HeadlessChrome` product token, or a brand
+// list reading "Chromium" identifies the client immediately, and a forgotten
+// flag would do exactly that. Failing closed is only correct when the closed
+// state is the safe one, and here it is not. So the knob defaults to on, and
+// only an explicit `=false` disables it.
+
+// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+// GL persona -- gated on --xrio-gl-persona.
+
+// True when a persona artifact parsed and its name is the one that was
+// selected. A value that does not match is ignored rather than partially
+// applied, so a typo cannot leave the strings spoofed and the capability table
+// honest.
+BASE_EXPORT bool GlPersonaActive();
+
+// Each returns true and overwrites `*value` when the persona covers `pname`.
+// False means pass the driver's own answer through untouched.
+//
+// These hook getParameter's four helpers rather than its switch: every
+// integer parameter reaches GetIntParameter, every float-array parameter
+// reaches GetWebGLFloatArrayParameter, and so on. Twelve values, four hooks --
+// and a parameter added upstream later routes through the same helpers, so the
+// persona covers it without a new patch.
+BASE_EXPORT bool OverrideGlInt(uint32_t pname, int32_t* value);
+BASE_EXPORT bool OverrideGlFloat(uint32_t pname, float* value);
+BASE_EXPORT bool OverrideGlFloatArray(uint32_t pname,
+                                      float* values,
+                                      size_t capacity);
+BASE_EXPORT bool OverrideGlIntArray(uint32_t pname,
+                                    int32_t* values,
+                                    size_t capacity);
+
+// UNMASKED_VENDOR_WEBGL / UNMASKED_RENDERER_WEBGL. nullptr when inactive.
+//
+// Spoofing these ALONE is a net loss and must never be shipped without the
+// table above: the renderer string would claim AMD while SUBPIXEL_BITS said 4
+// (catalog SS1 #5 corrected by SS3 #91). The strings and the capability
+// table therefore ship TOGETHER OR NOT AT ALL, which is what GlPersonaActive()
+// enforces -- a value that does not match is ignored rather than partially
+// applied.
+//
+// THE EXAMPLE HERE USED TO BE MAX_TEXTURE_SIZE, and it had to change on
+// 2026-09-08 because that limit is now deliberately left HONEST. Blink
+// validates draw calls against the limits it cached from the real driver, so a
+// spoofed MAX_TEXTURE_SIZE advertises 16384 and then refuses a 16384x1
+// texImage2D with INVALID_VALUE -- measured, along with MAX_RENDERBUFFER_SIZE
+// and MAX_SAMPLES doing the same. tools/gen_gl_persona.py WITHHOLDS those three
+// plus MAX_VIEWPORT_DIMS; see its WITHHELD set and
+// fork/gl-persona-capability-coverage.md section 6.
+//
+// The doctrine above is unchanged -- it is about shipping strings with NO table,
+// which GlPersonaActive() still enforces. What it does not distinguish is a
+// value that disagrees with a GPU DATABASE from one that disagrees with this
+// CONTEXT'S OWN BEHAVIOUR. The second kind is strictly louder: it needs no
+// reference machine.
+//
+// THE RULE: no value here may be one that something OTHER than getParameter
+// enforces. Stated precisely because the first version of this sentence
+// ("nothing in this table may be a limit of that kind") was false for the very
+// artifact regenerated alongside it.
+//
+// AND IT IS NEARLY TAUTOLOGICAL, which is the finding. A recapture on 2026-09-08
+// widened the parameter list by twelve and then measured, on this host, whether a
+// spoofed value survives contact with the operations it governs. Six of six
+// refuted, each using nothing but the context under test: a 15-block fragment
+// shader fails to LINK against a claimed 16, textureOffset(+20) fails to COMPILE
+// against a claimed 31 because the translator checks it, bindBufferRange at offset
+// 4 returns INVALID_VALUE against a claimed alignment of 4, and -- inverted -- a
+// 124-component program links even though the claim forbids it. The honest arm
+// agreed with itself on every one.
+//
+// So a getParameter hook cannot spoof a LIMIT, because a limit is by definition
+// something the implementation enforces somewhere else, and this hook is not
+// there. Twelve parameters are withheld for that reason. What the mechanism CAN
+// do is two things:
+//
+//   * values nothing checks -- SUBPIXEL_BITS and the interpolation-precision
+//     report; MAX_TEXTURE_LOD_BIAS and the ALIASED_*_RANGE pair, which are clamped
+//     rather than errored and are a declared residual (catching them needs a pixel
+//     measurement, which is weaker but not nothing);
+//   * HIDE extensions, which works precisely because
+//     ExtensionSupportedAndAllowed() changes the behaviour too -- the pnames go
+//     INVALID_ENUM and COMPRESSED_TEXTURE_FORMATS shrinks with them. That is the
+//     highest-leverage part of this whole mechanism and the only part that is
+//     coherent by construction.
+//
+// The corollary is the one fork/unbranded-tells.md section 3 already reaches by a
+// different route: a capability table cannot make SwiftShader into RENOIR, and the
+// fix is to render on the GPU the persona claims. See
+// fork/gl-persona-capability-coverage.md for the measurements.
+//
+// THE PIXELS ARE STILL THE ACTUAL RENDERER'S, AND THAT IS A KNOWN OPEN TELL.
+// The third leg of this doctrine used to be a seeded WebGL readback
+// perturbation, and this comment used to claim `perturb-gl-readback` defaulted
+// ON so "the three ship together or not at all". Both halves were false: the
+// registry said `false`, and the mechanism was removed on 2026-09-07. It was
+// measured to make things worse, not better -- `readPixels()` and a decoded
+// `gl.canvas.toDataURL()` read one framebuffer and disagreed on 378 of 11907
+// channel samples, which is a value no driver produces and which any page can
+// check with two lines and no reference machine. Perturbing did not make the
+// pixels hash like RENOIR; it made them hash like noise, trading a known-wrong
+// hash for an impossible one.
+//
+// So the premise stands and the prescription is gone: under SwiftShader a page
+// that hashes WebGL pixels gets SwiftShader's fleet-wide constant while these
+// strings claim RENOIR. That contradiction is OPEN, recorded in
+// fork/unbranded-tells.md §3, and the only honest fix is rendering on a GPU
+// that matches the claim -- a deployment question, not a hunk. See
+// fork/readback-perturbation.md for the full argument and the measurements.
+//
+// `const char*` rather than string_view so the call site can reuse Blink's
+// existing `String(const char*)` construction verbatim.
+//
+// FALLBACK CHAIN: `gl-vendor` / `gl-renderer` knob -> the persona artifact's own
+// field -> untouched. The artifact already carries both as data, so the knobs
+// are an override rather than a second source of truth, and an unset knob is the
+// artifact's value rather than a constant. Neither may be set without an active
+// persona -- ValidateConfig refuses the launch, and these accessors return
+// nullptr with no table regardless, so the fail-closed state is the same either
+// way.
+BASE_EXPORT const char* GlVendor();
+BASE_EXPORT const char* GlRenderer();
+
+// True for an extension the claimed GPU does not have. Which ones is artifact
+// data, not a compiled list -- currently the ASTC/ETC/ETC1 trio, a mobile-GPU
+// signature on a desktop Linux box, plus WEBGL_clip_cull_distance.
+//
+// Hiding only. Extensions the claimed GPU has and SwiftShader lacks are NOT
+// added: getExtension() would then hand back nothing, or a non-working object,
+// for something getSupportedExtensions() advertised -- a sharper contradiction
+// than a list three entries short.
+BASE_EXPORT bool GlExtensionHidden(std::string_view name);
+
+// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+// Machine profile -- gated on --xrio-seed AND --xrio-spoof-hardware.
+//
+// MEASURED on basharsx4, 3 fresh profiles: hardwareConcurrency 12 and
+// deviceMemory 8 on every one, along with 19 other constant surfaces. Constant
+// across fresh profiles is exactly what makes a value a join key.
+//
+// The three are drawn TOGETHER from one machine class, not independently, and
+// that coupling is the point: a 12-core box reporting 4 GB is not a machine
+// anyone owns, and an incoherent triple is a worse signal than a constant one.
+// The table itself is the `machine-classes` knob, so re-measuring it costs an
+// edit rather than a build -- and it stays ONE key for the same reason.
+//
+// THE STORAGE QUOTA IS NO LONGER PART OF IT. It was drawn from the same class
+// as a fraction of free disk, behind its own enable that defaulted off, and
+// was removed on 2026-09-29: static quota (kStaticStorageQuota, on at 152 and
+// 154, and in incognito from 154) reports usage + 10 GiB on any disk of 10 GiB
+// or more, so every drawn value was one no real Chrome reports, and upstream's
+// own answer is the right one. The class keeps its disk column because
+// `machine-class-expect` names it. fork/chrome-153-154-changelog-audit.md,
+// action 3.
+//
+// Each also accepts an outright override (`hardware-concurrency`,
+// `device-memory`) that bypasses the draw and needs no seed. ValidateConfig
+// refuses either beside `spoof-hardware=false` rather than letting a typed
+// value read as set and do nothing, which is the same rule `gl-vendor` without
+// `gl-persona` gets.
+// That does let an operator build the incoherent triple the coupling exists to
+// prevent, and that is deliberate: an explicit flag is a decision someone made
+// and the envelope records, whereas a silent draw is not. The coupling protects
+// against accident, not against intent.
+//
+// What is deliberately NOT varied, though it is equally constant: `platform`,
+// `vendor`, `oscpu`, `maxTouchPoints`, `pdfViewerEnabled`, the five-entry PDF
+// plugin list, and the Math results hash. Those describe a platform CLASS that
+// every real Linux Chrome shares -- varying them would not hide us among real
+// browsers, it would single us out from them. why-fork.md groups `math`,
+// `plugins` and `vendorFlavors` as fork-only work; on this evidence that is
+// wrong, and they belong with the should-stay-constant set.
+// WHETHER THIS HOST CAN HONOUR THE CLASS THE PROFILE WAS ALREADY DRAWN AS.
+// Returns a message when it cannot, and std::nullopt when there is nothing to
+// enforce or the placement is compatible.
+//
+// The defect it addresses: PickMachineClass() applies the ceiling to the WEIGHT
+// RENORMALISATION rather than to the drawn result, so the same seed selects a
+// different class on hosts of different size -- and cores and memory both
+// move with it. That is deliberate and documented at the draw, but it means
+// a session profile that migrates between host sizes is silently re-identified.
+//
+// A STATELESS VALIDATOR CANNOT DETECT THAT ON ITS OWN, which is the whole
+// shape of this: nothing in the fork knows what a previous host drew. The
+// expectation is an INPUT -- `machine-class-expect`, absent by default -- and
+// whatever creates the session profile persists it and passes it back. So this
+// closes the enforcement half and leaves the persistence half explicitly to
+// xrio; the item is not entirely local and fork/IMPLEMENTATION_ANSWERS.md
+// section 5 says so.
+//
+// It protects the DRAWN class only. An explicit `hardware-concurrency` or
+// `device-memory` override, or `spoof-hardware=false`, changes what a page
+// reads without tripping it.
+//
+// Both checks below run at browser startup after the GL artifact is installed,
+// so they compare against the live ceiling and the live draw.
+BASE_EXPORT std::optional<std::string> MachineClassExpectationProblem();
+
+// A configured `machine-classes` table with no row at or below
+// MachineClassCeiling(). The registry default is never judged.
+BASE_EXPORT std::optional<std::string> MachineClassTableProblem();
+
+BASE_EXPORT void SetHostThreadCeilingForTesting(unsigned ceiling);
+
+// The largest core count this host can honestly claim: its own processor
+// count. A page checks it with no reference machine at all -- spin N workers
+// and time them -- which is the same class of check that kept jitter-geometry
+// off, and it is why the draw is capped rather than merely weighted.
+BASE_EXPORT unsigned HostThreadCeiling();
+
+// HostThreadCeiling(), further capped by the active GL persona's max_threads
+// when one is installed. RENOIR's device id 0x164C is Lucienne, a Ryzen 5000U
+// mobile part: at most 16 threads, and only in laptops. A 24-core row under
+// that iGPU is a machine that was never built.
+BASE_EXPORT unsigned MachineClassCeiling();
+
+BASE_EXPORT std::optional<unsigned> HardwareConcurrency();
+BASE_EXPORT std::optional<float> DeviceMemoryGb();
+
+// performance.memory.jsHeapSizeLimit for the DRAWN class, or nullopt to leave
+// the host's own. Chrome-only, non-standard, still shipping, and a page can
+// compare it against navigator.deviceMemory with no reference machine.
+//
+// MEASURED AND THEN PROVED, in that order. V8 sizes the old generation as
+// clamp(physical / 2, 256 MB, 4 GB) on 64-bit
+// (heap.cc OldGenerationSizeFromPhysicalMemory), so ANY machine with 8 GB or
+// more gets exactly 4 GB; heap_size_limit is that plus young-generation terms,
+// so it always exceeds 4 GB. Blink then buckets it into 100 exponential
+// buckets topping out at "roughly 4GB" and SATURATES -- memory_info.cc's
+// QuantizeMemorySize returns bucket_size_list[kNumberOfBuckets - 1] for
+// anything larger. The top bucket is 3,760,000,000.
+//
+// So every machine at or above 8 GB reports the same number regardless of how
+// much RAM it really has, and every class in the default machine table is at or
+// above 8 GB. Confirmed 2026-09-04 on two hosts an order of magnitude apart --
+// a 15 GB box and a 125 GB box -- and on three different binaries: branded
+// Chrome 152.0.7977.75, our pristine 152.0.7977.64 and our fork. All three, both
+// hosts: 3760000000.
+//
+// The consequence is the whole item: there is no per-class table to capture,
+// and the only host that contradicts its own persona is one with LESS than
+// 8 GB, which reports a smaller bucket than any class in the table would. That
+// is the case this exists to fix.
+BASE_EXPORT std::optional<uint64_t> JsHeapSizeLimit();
+
+// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+// Battery status -- gated on `battery-status`, which defaults to ABSENT.
+//
+// EXPERIMENTAL, and the scope has to be read literally: this is one explicit,
+// operator-supplied, complete status tuple, held fixed for the life of the
+// process. It exists to answer whether the battery surface moves a cold
+// admission decision at all. It is NOT a fleet-safe battery model, and the two
+// differ in a way no amount of care in this file can close:
+//
+//   * A real battery MOVES. This one does not, so two renders from the same
+//     configuration report the same level forever -- a join key, i.e. the exact
+//     shape of defect the seeded surfaces exist to remove.
+//   * A restarted session profile resets nothing, because there is nothing to
+//     reset. A real laptop's level has advanced.
+//   * `machine-classes` carries cores, memory, free disk and a weight, and NO
+//     form factor -- so a battery claim cannot be drawn from the machine the
+//     rest of the persona claims. Turning this on says "laptop" while the
+//     machine class says nothing, and the coupling argument in the machine
+//     profile section above (a 12-core box reporting 4 GB is not a machine
+//     anyone owns) applies here with no table to appeal to.
+//
+// So it needs NO SEED, deliberately, and that is not an oversight: an explicit
+// tuple is a decision an operator made and the envelope records, exactly like
+// `hardware-concurrency` and `device-memory`, and every renderer launch already
+// carries a profile-derived seed -- so requiring one would gate nothing. The
+// fleet model that DOES need a seed (a laptop's state evolving from seed plus a
+// wall-clock anchor, drawn from a form factor the machine class supplies) is a
+// separate milestone and must not be mistaken for this one.
+//
+// PARSED ATOMICALLY, never as four independent knobs. Four booleans and doubles
+// would let an operator build `charging: true` with a finite discharging time --
+// a battery that is filling and emptying at once, which is louder than
+// reporting no battery at all. One JSON object, one validator, all four fields
+// or none.
+//
+// The hook is the DEVICE SERVICE (services/device/battery/), not Blink's
+// BatteryManager -- which is where the fork catalog's §5 #64 points, and it is
+// the wrong seam here. One transformation in the browser/device process covers
+// every renderer and every late subscriber, keeps the service's cached status
+// coherent with what pages were told, and leaves Blink's old/new comparison
+// operating on transformed values, so a host-only change cannot emit a
+// page-visible event while this override is fixed. A Blink hook would
+// duplicate configuration consumption per renderer and leave non-Blink battery
+// consumers reading the raw host.
+//
+// What a page observes for `level` is QUANTISED to two decimals, not this value
+// verbatim: blink::BatteryStatus::EnsureTwoSignificantDigits rounds it with
+// `std::round(level * 100) / 100`, deliberately, "reducing the possibility of
+// fingerprinting" and matching platforms that report 1% granularity. So a third
+// decimal is unobservable. Recorded here because a probe asserting an exact
+// `0.715` would otherwise read as a knob that did not apply -- and because a
+// tuple whose entropy lives in the third decimal is entropy no page can see.
+//
+// The two TIMES are not quantised. They cross as `base::Seconds(double)`, and
+// Infinity saturates to TimeDelta::Max(), which reports back as Infinity --
+// which is why "Infinity" is a spelling the knob has to accept rather than a
+// large finite stand-in.
+struct BatteryStatus {
+  bool charging = false;
+  // Seconds until full / until empty, as the Battery Status API reports them.
+  // Infinity means "not applicable or unknown" and is the value the API itself
+  // uses; JSON cannot spell it, so the knob accepts the string "Infinity".
+  double charging_time = 0.0;
+  double discharging_time = 0.0;
+  // (0, 1].
+  double level = 1.0;
+};
+
+// Parses the `battery-status` JSON object into one complete, coherent tuple.
+//
+// False with `error` set to something an operator can act on for anything else.
+// Exposed rather than kept private because the knob registry's validator calls
+// it: the coherence rules must exist once, or a launch-time check and a
+// call-site read can disagree about what a valid tuple is.
+//
+// THE RULES, each of which refuses a status no real device reports:
+//   * exactly the four keys, all present;
+//   * `level` in (0, 1] -- zero is a machine that has switched off;
+//   * each time a non-negative finite number, or the string "Infinity";
+//   * charging implies `discharging_time == Infinity`;
+//   * discharging implies `charging_time == Infinity`;
+//   * charging at `level == 1` implies `charging_time == 0` -- a full battery
+//     cannot have a finite non-zero time until full.
+BASE_EXPORT bool ParseBatteryStatus(std::string_view json,
+                                    BatteryStatus* out,
+                                    std::string* error);
+
+// The configured tuple, or nullopt for upstream behaviour byte for byte.
+//
+// FAILS CLOSED on a tuple that does not validate, which ValidateConfig has
+// already refused the launch over in the browser -- but not in a unit test or a
+// content_shell that never ran ChromeMainDelegate's startup hook, and those are
+// where the tests and the browsertest live. An incoherent tuple must leave the
+// host's own battery answer untouched there rather than reach a page.
+BASE_EXPORT std::optional<BatteryStatus> BatteryStatusOverride();
+
+// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+// Identity -- unconditional, i.e. the knob defaults to on.
+
+// navigator.webdriver has no legitimate consumer in this fleet, and answering in
+// C++ beats any driver-side handling: there is no getter to find, no descriptor
+// anomaly, no toString tell, and CDP cannot flip it back (catalog SS3 #87).
+BASE_EXPORT bool ForceWebdriverFalse();
+
+// Removes the "Headless" prefix from the product token. Scope this honestly: it
+// dissolves launcher.py's UA argument for headed-only operation and so lifts the
+// Xvfb display-slot ceiling on concurrency, but every other headless divergence
+// stands unmeasured. Throughput, not stealth (catalog SS3 #89, why-fork Tier 2).
+BASE_EXPORT bool SuppressHeadlessToken();
+
+// Sets `*brand` to the brand a branded Chrome reports. On by default because
+// this build compiles with CHROMIUM_BRANDING, where upstream leaves `brand`
+// unset and the list reduces to "Chromium" -- which announces the fork on the
+// first request. Two unrelated forks reached this independently (catalog SS1 #8,
+// SS3 #95).
+//
+// An EMPTY `ua-brand` is the off switch: `*brand` is left exactly as upstream
+// set it. That is spelled as an empty value rather than a separate bool because
+// there is nothing to say between "report this brand" and "report none".
+BASE_EXPORT void OverrideUserAgentBrand(std::optional<std::string>* brand);
+
+// The full version string to report on the high-entropy UA-CH surfaces, or
+// `real` when `ua-full-version` is unset.
+//
+// It exists because the fork's build number is not a released Chrome version.
+// `navigator.userAgent` is unaffected either way -- UA reduction pins it to
+// `<major>.0.0.0` -- so this reaches exactly three surfaces.
+BASE_EXPORT std::string UaFullVersionOr(std::string_view real);
+
+// Enables Blink's translate service so hrefTranslate is exposed. The upstream
+// renderer preference still enables it independently at the call site.
+BASE_EXPORT bool ExposeHrefTranslate();
+
+// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+// Outbound silence -- one predicate per behaviour.
+//
+// This was ONE function returning `true`, and that was a defect on its own:
+// `SuppressBackgroundFetches()` stood in for a variations seed fetch, a
+// network-time query, an intranet-redirect DNS probe AND the pref write that
+// goes with it, an outdated-build check, a first-run profile import and a
+// per-load DOM traversal. Six unrelated behaviours, three of which are not even
+// network traffic, and no way to change one without changing all six.
+//
+// Each now reads its own knob, and each of those defaults to
+// `suppress-background-fetches` -- so one flag still turns the group off, and a
+// single flag can now turn one member back on.
+//
+// Suppressing in-binary beats blocking at the tunnel, and the difference is not
+// cosmetic: a request the relay refuses has already cost a connection out of the
+// proxy's budget, which is the real ceiling on our concurrency. Relabel the
+// benefit honestly, though -- this is a proxy-connection-budget item, not
+// detection work (catalog SS1 #34).
+BASE_EXPORT bool SuppressVariationsFetch();
+BASE_EXPORT bool SuppressNetworkTimeQuery();
+BASE_EXPORT bool SuppressIntranetRedirectProbe();
+BASE_EXPORT bool SuppressOutdatedBuildDetector();
+BASE_EXPORT bool SuppressFirstRunImport();
+
+// The four the 2026-08-20 NetLog capture found, ordered by measured volume.
+// Every one of them survives --disable-background-networking,
+// --disable-component-update and --disable-domain-reliability, which between
+// them remove 8 requests of 52; the belief that our launch ledger already
+// handled background traffic was simply false.
+//
+// 16 of 58 requests on one page load: android.clients.google.com/checkin and
+// /c2dm/register3. Gated at GCMDriverDesktop::EnsureStarted, which is the one
+// point both endpoints pass through and which already has a non-SUCCESS early
+// return, so nothing is left half-initialised.
+BASE_EXPORT bool SuppressGcm();
+
+// 12 of 58: update.googleapis.com/service/update2/json and the
+// edgedl.me.gvt1.com/edgedl/diffgen-puffin payload behind it. Gated inside
+// update_client, BELOW the component updater, the extensions updater and the
+// updater scheduler alike -- so it does not matter which of them still pings.
+BASE_EXPORT bool SuppressUpdatePings();
+
+// 4 (+2 CDN): redirector.gvt1.com/edgedl/chrome/dict/*.bdic. The download only;
+// the `spellcheck` attribute and IDL property are Blink-side and untouched, so
+// suppressing the fetch does not disable the feature a page can observe.
+BASE_EXPORT bool SuppressDictionaryDownload();
+
+// 4: accounts.google.com/ListAccounts, from the account reconcilor on a profile
+// that has never signed in. Stock sends it too, so this is not a fork tell --
+// it is suppressed for the connection budget, like the three above.
+BASE_EXPORT bool SuppressAccountReconcile();
+
+// Renderer-side, and therefore not like the nine above: it removes a traversal
+// of the loaded document, so it is a timing and behaviour change on every page
+// load rather than a suppressed request.
+BASE_EXPORT bool SuppressFrameworkDetection();
+
+// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+// Automation ergonomics -- likewise split, from one `return true` covering
+// three page-visible behaviours. Each defaults to
+// `suppress-automation-blockers`.
+
+// beforeunload dialogs stall the automation channel outright until something
+// dismisses them.
+BASE_EXPORT bool SuppressBeforeunloadDialogs();
+
+// And what to answer when one is suppressed: true leaves the page, false stays
+// and cancels the navigation. Outside the umbrella because it is an answer, not
+// a suppression -- turning the suppression off makes it moot rather than
+// inverting it.
+BASE_EXPORT bool BeforeunloadAutoAccept();
+
+// window.open() lands in a foreground tab rather than a popup window. Genuinely
+// page-visible: a real popup reports different outerWidth/outerHeight and
+// carries popup chrome.
+BASE_EXPORT bool PopupOpensAsForegroundTab();
+
+// Startup infobars consume viewport and change what the page is laid out into --
+// the bar that makes chrome-for-testing unusable, and the didn't-shut-down-
+// cleanly bubble that one-process-per-render invites (catalog SS1 #24, #26).
+BASE_EXPORT bool SuppressStartupInfobars();
+
+// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+// Headless permission semantics. SHIPS OFF.
+
+// Whether a permission request in headless reaches Chrome's normal prompt path
+// instead of the unconditional denial upstream applies whenever the screen is
+// headless (components/permissions/permission_request_manager.cc:219 -- second
+// guard in AddRequest, no feature flag, no content-settings type filter).
+//
+// This is the one identity-shaped row that deliberately defaults FALSE, and the
+// asymmetry is the point. The other three default true because their closed
+// state is the dangerous one: a forgotten flag would ship `webdriver === true`
+// or a `HeadlessChrome` token. Here the closed state is "deny" -- a tell, but a
+// safe one -- while the OPEN state routes into Views prompt construction whose
+// first statement is CHECK(GetNativeWindow()). A tell is worse than nothing; a
+// CHECK is worse than a tell.
+//
+// Enabling it reproduces headed behaviour exactly, INCLUDING that an undecided
+// request never settles and the page's promise waits for the render timeout.
+// That is what parity means on this surface, not a defect -- and it is why this
+// is a per-target retreat knob rather than a fleet-wide switch.
+//
+// It does not invent an answer. Explicit policies still win and are unchanged:
+// --deny-permission-prompts is checked BEFORE this guard, and a content setting
+// (granted or blocked) is resolved by PermissionContextBase and never reaches
+// AddRequest at all. Chrome's own state machine produces the result.
+BASE_EXPORT bool HeadfulPermissionPromptsEnabled();
+
+// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+// WebRTC.
+
+// The value the WebRTC IP-handling pref is REGISTERED with, so it applies with
+// no flag present at all. Measured: the pref, not the switch, is what suppresses
+// ICE gathering, and a default cannot be forgotten at launch the way an argv
+// entry can (catalog SS1 #22).
+//
+// The four upstream values are exactly the four IP-leak postures, which is what
+// makes this the highest-value knob in the fork: changing posture used to cost a
+// five-hour rebuild. Returns std::string, not string_view -- the knob accessor
+// returns a temporary and a view into it would dangle.
+BASE_EXPORT std::string WebRtcIpHandlingPolicy();
+
+// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+// Display and theme.
+
+BASE_EXPORT std::optional<int> WorkAreaPanelHeight();
+BASE_EXPORT std::optional<bool> ColorSchemeIsDark();
+
+// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+// Per-surface perturbation enables.
+//
+// These exist so a ruleset can retreat on ONE surface for ONE target instead of
+// retreating fleet-wide. That matters because a globally-retreated surface is
+// exactly how a fleet-wide constant gets reintroduced: "turn the GL persona off
+// because target X breaks" becomes "turn it off for X".
+//
+// They are gates on top of the seed, not replacements for it: no seed still
+// means every one of these surfaces is untouched.
+//
+// THE READBACK GATE ALSO REQUIRES AN ACTIVE GL PERSONA. Perturbed pixels
+// underneath an honest `SwiftShader Device (Subzero) (0x0000C0DE)` renderer
+// string is a machine no driver produces, and `xrio --chrome-path` without
+// `--gl-persona` reached exactly that state through an ordinary flag
+// combination. Gating here rather than in xrio because this one cannot be
+// bypassed.
+//
+// THERE USED TO BE TWO. `perturb-gl-readback` was removed on 2026-09-07 --
+// measured, not argued: its readPixels hook disagreed with the unhooked
+// toDataURL path on 378 of 11907 channel samples reading one framebuffer, and a
+// perturbation two paths disagree about is a stronger fingerprint than the
+// constant hash it covered. fork/readback-perturbation.md carries the numbers.
+//
+// `perturb-canvas` additionally defaults OFF; see its knob doc. `PerturbCanvas`
+// covers getImageData AND the toDataURL/toBlob encode path, deliberately one
+// knob: split them and a configuration exists where the two disagree about the
+// same pixels, which is louder than either constant hash alone -- which is
+// exactly the defect that retired the WebGL gate.
+//
+// AND SO DOES `perturb-audio`, SINCE 2026-09-09, along with both of its
+// children. Same argument, one surface over: `ScaleAudioChannel` multiplies
+// EVERY sample of a completed buffer by one scalar, so an OfflineAudioContext
+// rendering a signal the page already knows -- a DC constant, or a unity-gain
+// pass-through of its own input -- hands that input back scaled by a number no
+// node in the graph applied. Silence is NOT one of those signals, and
+// tools/audio_probe.py says so where it measures it: zero times the scale is
+// still zero, so a silent buffer cannot show this and is carried as a negative
+// control instead. The page holds the ground truth, the
+// same way creepjs holds it for canvas, and needs no reference machine to read
+// the answer. fork/IMPLEMENTATION_CHECKLIST.md section 3.
+//
+// The two audio gates deliberately do NOT AND with anything else, unlike
+// PerturbCanvasEnabled(): audio makes no claim about a GPU, so an active GL
+// persona is not a precondition. The seed still is, and that gate lives in
+// ScaleAudioChannel() rather than here.
+BASE_EXPORT bool PerturbCanvasEnabled();
+BASE_EXPORT bool PerturbAudioBuffersEnabled();
+BASE_EXPORT bool PerturbOfflineAudioEnabled();
+BASE_EXPORT bool JitterTextMetricsEnabled();
+
+}  // namespace base::xrio
+
+#endif  // BASE_XRIO_PERSONA_H_
